    Add migration flow, topic volume, and the resolution loop
    
    Three views the demo needed, and the mock data to exercise them.
    
    Migration flow: publicly stated moves to and from competing products, as a
    diverging bar per competitor. Direction is stored rather than inferred, since
    reading it backwards would invert the most consequential number on the page,
    and each row keeps the verbatim quote that justifies its direction. Moves
    stated only as intent ("might switch", "still deciding") are shown but
    excluded from net, because counting an intention as a migration inflates
    churn. Rows sort by net so the tools taking users cluster together — an
    aggregate churn figure would average them into one number and hide that a
    single competitor accounts for most departures, for one coherent reason.
    
    Topic volume: discussion per product topic over time, stacked, so attention
    moving between features is visible rather than reconstructed. Largest total
    sits on the baseline where a stacked band reads accurately.
    
    Resolution loop: the audit trail from a public complaint to the reporter
    agreeing it is fixed. The ordering is the contract — a fix is not announced
    before it exists and a test guards it, and nothing but the reporter's own
    confirmation closes an issue. The seeded data includes one loop that stops at
    "asked them to confirm" and stays open, because a dashboard that counted that
    as resolved would be lying about the one thing the flow promises. Outgoing
    messages are quoted in full: a reply posted in the company's name is what an
    audit needs to reread.
    
    Discord and LinkedIn become first-class venues, which needed the Venue union,
    the schema enum, the server's URL mapping, and two more categorical palette
    slots across all three theme blocks.
    
